The risk is that your app currently has two different filters handling Bearer JWTs:

your custom JwtAuthenticationFilter, which puts an AuthenticatedUser into the SecurityContext
Spring Security’s built-in BearerTokenAuthenticationFilter, which usually puts a Jwt into the SecurityContext
If Spring’s built-in filter runs after your custom filter, it may overwrite the authentication you set.

Then controller methods like:

java
Run Code

Copy code
@AuthenticationPrincipal AuthenticatedUser user


may fail, because the actual principal is a Jwt, not an AuthenticatedUser.

The problem is not proven yet because no test currently sends a real authenticated HTTP request.

So everything may look fine in code, but the first real frontend call to a protected endpoint could break.